# C++ ImGui Project Workflow (Windows + CMake + MinGW)

This is the step-by-step process for creating and running a new C++ GUI project using Dear ImGui + GLFW + CMake.

---

# 1. Create a new project folder

Inside my main workspace:

kevinachkar_codes/
 ├── Main_Code/   (working template)
 ├── pid_tuner/    (new project)

Each project = ONE folder.

---

# 2. Copy template (VERY IMPORTANT)

From a working project (imgui_test), copy into the new folder:

✔ imgui/
✔ glfw/
✔ CMakeLists.txt
✔ main.cpp

So new project looks like:

pid_tuner/
 ├── main.cpp
 ├── CMakeLists.txt
 ├── imgui/
 ├── glfw/

---

# 3. Rename project (optional but recommended)

Open CMakeLists.txt and change:

project(pid_tuner)

add_executable(pid_tuner main.cpp ...)

(This just names the executable properly)

---

# 4. Write my code

All coding happens in:

main.cpp

This is where I:
- add ImGui sliders
- build UI
- connect logic (PID, robot arm, etc.)

---

# 5. Configure build (IMPORTANT FIX)

Open terminal inside the project folder:

cd C:\Users\kevin\OneDrive\Desktop\kevinachkar_codes\ImGui\folder name

⚠ ALWAYS use this command (IMPORTANT):

cmake -S . -B build -G "MinGW Makefiles"

DO NOT use //only:
cmake -B build
(because it may incorrectly choose NMake)

This step generates build files using MinGW correctly.

---

# 6. Build project (compile step)

cmake --build build

This turns C++ code into an executable (.exe)

---

# 7. Run program

Run the generated executable:

.\build\pid_tuner.exe

---

# 8. What CMake does (IMPORTANT IDEA)

CMake = builder system

It:
- reads CMakeLists.txt
- finds source files
- tells compiler what to compile
- links ImGui + GLFW
- produces .exe file

---

# 9. What g++ does

g++ = compiler

It:
- converts .cpp → machine code
- creates object files
- links everything into .exe

---

# 10. What VS Code is

VS Code = editor only

It:
- does NOT build the project
- does NOT run the code
- only helps me write code

---

# 11. Simple workflow summary

Every time I code:

1. Write code in main.cpp
2. Configure project:
   cmake -S . -B build -G "MinGW Makefiles"
3. Build project:
   cmake --build build
4. Run:
   .\build\project.exe

---

# 12. Important rule

ONE PROJECT = ONE FOLDER

Do NOT reinstall ImGui or GLFW for every project.

Just copy the folder template.

---

# 13. My future uses for this setup

- PID tuning GUI for drones
- Robot arm control panel
- IMU real-time visualizer
- Serial communication tools (Arduino / ESP32)
- ML / robotics dashboards